## 3️⃣ Adding real input validation (example)

If you later introduce a route that accepts user data, you should **never trust** the request as‑is. Below is a tiny illustration using **Joi** (you can replace it with Zod or any validator you prefer).

```js
// ----- NEW DEPENDENCY -------------------------------------------------
const Joi = require('joi');

// ----- Example POST /users -------------------------------------------------
app.post('/users', (req, res, next) => {
  const schema = Joi.object({
    name: Joi.string().trim().min(2).max  (100).required(),
    email: Joi.string().email({ tlds: { allow: false } }).required(),
    // any other fields…
  });

  const { error, value } = schema.validate(req.body, { abortEarly: false });

  if (error) {
    // Convert Joi details into a nicer JSON response
    const details = error.details.map((d) => d.message);
    return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Invalid request', details });
  }

  // Safe to use `value` (sanitised & typed)
  // …create user in DB, etc.
  res.status(201).json({ id: 123, ...value });
});
```

